 1️⃣ What is Saga Design Pattern (Recap)

> Saga is a pattern to manage distributed transactions across multiple microservices without 2PC, using local transactions + compensation.

Each step:
- Commits locally
- Publishes something
- Next service reacts
- On failure → compensating actions run

 2️⃣ Core Question: Saga is Event-based or Message-based?

👉 Answer:  
> Saga is BOTH — but the best practice is EVENT-DRIVEN

Let’s clarify clearly.

 2.1 Saga using Messages (Command-based Saga)

 Characteristics
- Direct commands
- Tighter coupling
- Orchestrator usually present

 Example

OrderService → PaymentService
              "ChargePayment"

📌 This is message-based (command)

 2.2 Saga using Events (Event-based Saga) ✅ (Preferred)

 Characteristics
- Loose coupling
- Choreography-based
- Services react to facts

 Example

OrderPlaced → PaymentService listens
PaymentCompleted → Inventory listens

📌 This is event-driven Saga

 2.3 Quick Comparison

| Aspect | Message-based | Event-based |
|--------|---------------|-------------|
| Coupling | High | Low |
| Scalability | Medium | High |
| Extensibility | Hard | Easy |
| Style | Orchestration | Choreography |
| Preferred | ❌ | ✅ |
 
 3️⃣ Two Types of Saga Patterns

 1️⃣ Choreography Saga (Event-driven) ✅
- No central coordinator
- Each service reacts to events

 2️⃣ Orchestration Saga (Command-driven)
- Central saga orchestrator
- Sends commands
 
